[USER]:
1	# 📱 MCP Server for iOS Simulator
2	[![smithery badge](https://smithery.ai/badge/@atom2ueki/mcp-server-ios-simulator)](https://smithery.ai/server/@atom2ueki/mcp-server-ios-simulator)
3	
4	A server that implements the Model Context Protocol (MCP) for iOS simulators, built on top of [appium-ios-simulator](https://github.com/appium/appium-ios-simulator) and utilizing the [MCP TypeScript SDK](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk).
5	
6	<a href="https://glama.ai/mcp/servers/@atom2ueki/mcp-server-ios-simulator">
7	  <img width="380" height="200" src="https://glama.ai/mcp/servers/@atom2ueki/mcp-server-ios-simulator/badge" alt="Server for iOS Simulator MCP server" />
8	</a>
9	
10	## 📋 Overview
11	
12	This project provides a bridge between iOS simulators and the Model Context Protocol, allowing for standardized communication with iOS simulator instances. It enables programmatic control of iOS simulators while leveraging the MCP protocol for consistent interfaces across different environments. The server utilizes stdio as its transport mechanism, making it ideal for integration with Claude Desktop and other MCP-compatible clients.
13	
14	## 🎬 Demo
15	
16	![iOS Simulator Demo](demo/demo1.gif)
17	
18	*Demo showing how to boot an iOS simulator using Claude AI Desktop*
19	
20	## 🏗️ Architecture
21	
22	The server consists of three main components:
23	
24	1. **🔄 Simulator Management Layer** - Handles iOS simulator lifecycle and interactions
25	2. **🔌 MCP Protocol Implementation** - Implements the Model Context Protocol using the TypeScript SDK with stdio transport
26	3. **📊 Logger Component** - Provides file-based logging without interfering with the stdio transport
27	
28	```
29	┌─────────────────┐     ┌─────────────────┐     ┌─────────────────┐
30	│  MCP Protocol   │     │     Stdio       │     │    Simulator    │
31	│  Implementation │◄────┤    Transport    │◄────┤   Management    │
32	│                 │     │                 │     │      Layer      │
33	└─────────────────┘     └─────────────────┘     └─────────────────┘
34	        ▲                                                ▲
35	        │                                                │
36	        ▼                                                ▼
37	┌─────────────────┐                             ┌─────────────────┐
38	│   MCP Client    │                             │  iOS Simulator  │
39	│  (e.g. Claude)  │                             │                 │
40	└─────────────────┘                             └─────────────────┘
41	```
42	
43	## ✨ Features
44	
45	- 🚀 Start, stop, and manage iOS simulator instances
46	- 🔌 Boot and shutdown simulators
47	- 📲 Install and launch applications on simulators
48	- 📸 Take screenshots of simulator screens
49	- 👆 Perform taps on coordinates
50	- 🔄 Support for multiple concurrent simulator sessions
51	- 📝 Comprehensive file-based logging without console output
52	- 🛡️ Error-resilient operation
53	
54	## 📋 Prerequisites
55	
56	- 🟢 Node.js (v16 or later)
57	- 🍎 macOS (required for iOS simulators)
58	- 🛠️ Xcode with iOS simulators installed
59	- 📜 TypeScript 4.5+
60	
61	## 🔧 Installation
62	
63	### Installing via Smithery
64	
65	To install iOS Simulator Control Server for Claude Desktop automatically via [Smithery](https://smithery.ai/server/@atom2ueki/mcp-server-ios-simulator):
66	
67	```bash
68	npx -y @smithery/cli install @atom2ueki/mcp-server-ios-simulator --client claude
69	```
70	
71	### Manual Installation
72	```bash
73	# Clone the repository
74	git clone https://github.com/atom2ueki/mcp-server-ios-simulator.git
75	cd mcp-server-ios-simulator
76	
77	# Install dependencies
78	npm install
79	```
80	
81	## ⚙️ Configuration
82	
83	Configuration is handled through the `src/config.ts` file:
84	
85	```typescript
86	const config = {
87	  simulator: {
88	    defaultDevice: process.env.SIMULATOR_DEFAULT_DEVICE || 'iPhone 16',
89	    defaultOS: process.env.SIMULATOR_DEFAULT_OS || '18.2',
90	    timeout: parseInt(process.env.SIMULATOR_TIMEOUT || '30000', 10),
91	  }
92	};
93	```
94	
95	You can customize these settings by setting environment variables:
96	
97	```
98	SIMULATOR_DEFAULT_DEVICE=iPhone 16
99	SIMULATOR_DEFAULT_OS=18.2
100	SIMULATOR_TIMEOUT=30000
101	```
102	
103	## 🚀 Usage
104	
105	### 🔨 Building and Starting the Server
106	
107	```bash
108	# Build the project
109	npm run build
110	
111	# Start the server
112	npm start
113	```
114	
115	### 🧰 MCP Tools
116	
117	The server provides two distinct approaches for controlling iOS simulators:
118	
119	#### 📱 Direct Simulator Management (Recommended)
120	These tools work directly with simulator UDIDs and don't require maintaining sessions:
121	
122	- 📋 `list-available-simulators` - List all available simulators with their UDIDs
123	- ▶️ `boot-simulator-by-udid` - Boot a simulator directly using its UDID
124	- ⏹️ `shutdown-simulator-by-udid` - Shutdown a simulator directly using its UDID
125	- 📊 `list-booted-simulators` - List all currently booted simulators
126	
127	**Use this approach when:** You just want to boot, use, and shut down simulators directly.
128	
129	#### 📱 Session-Based Management (Advanced)
130	These tools use a session layer that tracks simulators with custom session IDs:
131	
132	- 📋 `list-simulator-sessions` - List all active simulator sessions
133	- ➕ `create-simulator-session` - Create a new simulator session
134	- ❌ `terminate-simulator-session` - Terminate a session (shuts down simulator and cleans up)
135	- 🔄 `create-and-boot-simulator` - Create a new simulator session and boot it
136	- ▶️ `boot-simulator` - Boot a simulator for an existing session
137	- ⏹️ `shutdown-simulator` - Shutdown a simulator for an existing session
138	
139	**Use this approach when:** You need to track simulator metadata, reference simulators by custom IDs, or use the more advanced management features.
140	
141	#### 📲 Application Management
142	- 📥 `install-app` - Install an application on a simulator
143	- 🚀 `launch-app` - Launch an application on a simulator
144	- 🛑 `terminate-app` - Terminate a running application on a simulator
145	
146	#### 🖱️ Interaction Tools
147	- 📷 `take-screenshot` - Take a screenshot of the simulator screen
148	- 👆 `tap-coordinate` - Perform a tap at the specified coordinates
149	
150	### 🤖 Example Usage with Claude Desktop
151	
152	1. Configure Claude Desktop to use this server as an MCP tool:
153	   - Open Claude Desktop
154	   - Go to Settings > Advanced
155	   - Add the following configuration to the "MCP Servers" section:
156	
157	   ```json
158	   {
159	     "mcpServers": {
160	       "simulator": {
161	         "command": "node",
162	         "args": [
163	           "/path/to/your/mcp-server-ios-simulator/dist/index.js"
164	         ]
165	       }
166	     }
167	   }
168	   ```
169	
170	   - Replace `/path/to/your` with the actual path to where you've installed this repository
171	   - Save the settings and restart Claude Desktop
172	
173	2. Use the provided tools to control iOS simulators directly from Claude Desktop:
174	   
175	   **Direct UDID Approach (Recommended):**
176	   1. First, ask Claude to list available simulators:
177	      ```
178	      "Show me all available iOS simulators"
179	      ```
180	   
181	   2. Then use the UDID to boot a specific simulator:
182	      ```
183	      "Boot the iOS simulator with UDID 5272EA61-5796-4372-86FE-3B33831D5CC1"
184	      ```
185	   
186	   3. When finished, shut it down using the same UDID:
187	      ```
188	      "Shut down the simulator with UDID 5272EA61-5796-4372-86FE-3B33831D5CC1"
189	      ```
190	   
191	   The direct UDID approach is simpler and more reliable for most use cases.
192	   
193	   **Session-Based Approach (Advanced):**
194	   Only use this approach if you need the advanced features of session tracking:
195	   ```
196	   "Create a new simulator session for iPhone 16 Pro with iOS 18.2"
197	   "Boot the simulator for session abc-123"
198	   "Take a screenshot of the simulator for session abc-123"
199	   "Terminate the simulator session abc-123"
200	   ```
